 @MrHenrique525  And DLSS is totally exclusive to Nvidia cards with RTX? What's your point?
AMD now did for the first time with FSR4 what Nvidia did with DLSS from the beginning.
Otherwise some features are missing, like (Multi)-Frame Gen on newer cards.

 @arkhos3367  So the guy who made the video straight-up says the 7400F would give the exact same FPS, and yet you’re here parroting textbook benchmark jargon like it’s gospel. Congrats, you just gave a passionate defense of a CPU that didn’t need to be used in the first place.

You’re not defending accuracy, you’re defending wasted effort. The whole point of a "comparison" is to help buyers, not show off what a midrange GPU can do when it’s strapped to a CPU no one actually owns. If performance is identical on a $180 CPU, then using a $500 one is nothing but benchmark theater for clout-chasing youtubers and comment section bootlickers.

But sure, keep pretending it’s all about "data integrity" while the reviewer himself just admitted it made no difference. Absolute comedy.

 @Sttav   Since both CPU's give the same result then obviously use the one that gives comparable data. You're defeating your own point. And it is about data integrity. That's why everybody does it because it is the bottom line in what is comparable. It is fascinating that you're so bitter about basic integrity.

What you call "benchmark jargon" is in fact gospel when talking about benchmarking, yes. That should be obvious.

The 9800X3D is the top selling CPU for multiple vendors in my country. Saying no one owns it is an outright lie. Or perhaps you don't know what you're talking about.

 @arkhos3367  You admit the 7400F gives the same results, then defend using a $500 CPU "for integrity"? 

"Benchmark gospel"? No one cares if the data is "pure" if it’s useless for actual buyers.

Also, not once did I say no one buys the 9800X3D. I said people buying midrange GPUs don’t pair them with $500 CPUs. Learn to read before vomiting out condescending walls of text.

 @arkhos3367  Yeah, I said “CPU no one actually owns” in the context of pairing it with a midrange GPU, which any non-braindead person could understand. Reading comprehension is free.
You’re calling my points circular while repeating the same recycled CPU-worship logic for the 20th time. A 9800X3D with a 5060 Ti is a clown build, and no amount of “data integrity” buzzwords will make it less ridiculous.

If ignoring reality is sound methodology, then I’m glad I’m not the target audience.

 @cbrunhaver  It's called DirectSR but has only been around for a year in preview so it doesn't ship in any games yet.
Not like Vulkan has anything like that anyway, so if anything Microsoft is in the lead here.

A lot of games today are using NVidia Streamline, which despite being NVidia's SDK, supports XeSS and FSR and has become the standard for now.

 @CodeX-f6b No, DLSS4 Performance mode allows you to use the 5060ti on a 4K screen in any game supporting DLSS, while with the 9060 you have to use FSR2/3 on most older games which is a significant hit to image quality. Or makes 4K basically unplayable if you have to stick to native rendering. The issue in this case is that 4K with FSR performance mode doesnt look better than 1440p with quality mode. So using 4K becomes pointless, however With DLSS4 4K performance mode even is a huge upgrade compared to native 1440p rendering.
So there are many scenarios where the AMD card isnt even feasible at all… But even in any other usecase the lacking FSR4 support in older games really is a huge disappointment because even with todays GPUs you still need Upscaling to get decent framerates. 

FSR4 on its own is very good, but AMD totally dropped the ball with backwards compatiblity. Its such a massive advantage on nvidia side, taht I would even pay double the price for it. Upscaling is ESSENTIAL these days.
